2026, day 107

Good morning from Asia.

‘Trump says Iran has agreed to hand over its enriched uranium supply’ (AFP);

‘Trump says next round of US-Iran talks could happen over weekend’ (FT);

‘Iran-US talks turn to interim deal amid rifts over nuclear work, Iranian sources say’ (Reuters);

‘US-Iran Deal Will Take Months, Gulf and Europe Officials Say’ (BBG);

‘Cease-Fire Between Israel and Lebanon Takes Effect’ (WSJ);

‘US moves to sideline Iran in Lebanon, but the conflicts remain intertwined’ (Al Arabiya);

‘Naval Blockades Have Won Wars but Sometimes at Great Cost’ (WSJ) - “From ancient Greece to the Cold War, belligerents have fought for control over shipping”;

‘US military widens blockade on Iran to include contraband shipments, advisory says’ (Reuters);

‘US oil bosses warn Donald Trump to block Iran’s Strait of Hormuz toll’ (FT);

‘US forces ready to strike Iran’s power plants, energy industry if ordered: Hegseth’ (Reuters);

‘How the U.S. Is Uniting Libyan Rivals to Squeeze Out Russia’ (WSJ) - “Peace could unlock oil and mineral wealth, as well as expanding American influence”;

‘Trump again lashes Australia over Hormuz as Chalmers warns of dangers’ (AFR);

‘Defence spending to lift by $53 billion over the next decade as government warns of 'intensifying' risks’ (ABC);

‘Labor to tap super funds for $53b defence build-up’ (AFR);

‘Rachel Reeves warns other budgets may be cut to lift defence spending’ (Guardian);

‘Nato in ‘turf war’ with EU over defence spending’ (FT) - “At heart of dispute is buying US weapons with EU money”;

‘Russia plans to put nuclear weapons in orbit for ‘space Pearl Harbor’’ (Times) - “Kremlin could cause mayhem by interfering with civilian aircraft and satellites”;

‘Russia threatens European drone producers, publishes addresses online’ (Euractiv) - “Joint production ventures in Europe are "potential targets" for Russia”;

‘Japan eyes bolstered defense of Pacific sea lanes to US, Australia, Asia’ (Nikkei Asia) - “Hormuz oil disruption spurs Tokyo to consider more secure fuel, food supplies”;

‘Residents living on London's new Chinese embassy site fear they have unknowingly been living 'in China' since 2018’ (Sky);

‘Cuba Tried to Deliver Secret Letter Directly to Trump Via Businessman’ (WSJ);

‘ Pentagon ramps up planning for possible military ops in Cuba’ (USA Today);

‘Pope: World is being ravaged by tyrants’ (Telegraph);

In geoeconomics, ‘U.S. to Create High-Tech Manufacturing Zone in Philippines’ (WSJ) - “Hub will aim to forge China-proof supply chains with automated factories and local resources”;

‘Britain’s finally getting a Trump insurance policy. Just don’t expect Starmer to use it’ (Politico) - “New powers under consideration could include restrictions on the import and export of goods and services, as well as on investment flows”;

‘Chef José Andrés warns of multi-year world famine from Iran war’ (Guardian);

‘China willing to ‘buy’ or ‘steal’ US tech to get ahead in AI race, Congress told’ (SCMP);

In politics, ‘Fed chair nominee Warsh's disclosures don't comply with Senate ethics rules, Senator Warren says’ (Reuters);

‘US State Department restricts visas for those who ‘support adversaries’’ (Al Jazeera);

‘SCOTUS rancor hits fever pitch’ (Axios) - “Supreme Court justices are trading barbs over the court's direction, breaking from their usually private and civil decorum”;

‘Thomas warns intolerance among younger generations will ‘infect’ courts’ (The Hill);

‘Starmer sacks Foreign Office chief over Mandelson vetting scandal’ (Telegraph);

‘Boost for women — or Modi? India plans world’s biggest elected parliament’ (FT) - “Largest democratic overhaul in more than five decades comes as population surges”;

In markets, ‘The Iran war has shattered oil's price compass’ (Reuters) - “Physical crude is trading at record highs while futures benchmarks signal calm”;

‘Why biggest threat to US’ global dollar dominance may well be Washington itself’ (SCMP)
